 en:{image:'./assets/projects/chroma/pro-en.png',category:'SIGNAL NOTES / CHROMA',title:'A little colour for the heartbeat of a computer.',summary:'Electric blue, yellow, signal red and a little chrome. CHROMA is a hardware observatory for my computer, and a way to bring a Y2K idea of the future onto the desktop.',
 lead:'I like exploring computer hardware, and I’m curious about what it is doing: what is keeping the CPU busy, how warm is the GPU, and where did the memory go? Useful questions, but I wanted the place where I read the answers to have some character too. So I made CHROMA. A local hardware observatory with the colour turned up and the measurements kept clear.',
 quote:'Let the colour be loud.\nLet the signals be honest.',imageAlt:'Actual installed CHROMA 0.1.0 PRO workspace in English',caption:'CAPTURE 01 / Actual installed application. These are readings from the developer’s computer at capture time, not your device.',
 sections:[
 {title:'Turn the colour up',paragraphs:['This time, I didn’t want a quiet-looking tool. Electric blue holds everything together, yellow pulls your attention closer, and signal red takes over a whole panel. A little cyan joins in. Silver frames feel like instrument enclosures; raised buttons, pixel lettering and rows of tiny tiles suggest a signal just coming online.','That is the Y2K future I wanted to borrow: bold, playful, and convinced that computers could still become more colourful. A scan line passes, light travels across metal, and a star turns in a corner. A serious tool can look like it is enjoying itself.']},
 {title:'A glance, then a closer look',paragraphs:['Simple mode enlarges the CPU/GPU utilization and temperatures, with memory, VRAM and network information nearby. Opening the app does not have to mean reading a wall of sensor names.','PRO goes further: logical threads, clocks, power, memory modules, disks, adapters, and sensors you can search and favorite. A small always-on-top desktop window keeps a few readings close. Exclusive full-screen games may cover it; this version does not provide an injected in-game OSD.'],image:'./assets/projects/chroma/mini.png',imageAlt:'Actual CHROMA desktop mini view with CPU, GPU and memory readings',caption:'CAPTURE 02 / MINI VIEW. Less information, closer at hand. Values represent capture time only.'},
 {title:'Leave room for a missing number',paragraphs:['For a hardware monitor, the important question is where the readings come from. CHROMA collects locally. CPU utilization uses system counters, while sensors depend on the hardware, drivers and access available. An unavailable temperature stays empty. Stale sensor streams expire. There is no plausible-looking number added just to fill a panel.','The frame lab integrates PresentMon and lets you target a process for actual application-frame data. Permission and error paths have been checked, and the calculations have tests. Actual in-game FPS, additional hardware and long stress scenarios still need verification. A “CPU limited” label is a clue, not a definitive diagnosis.','Sessions export to CSV or JSON. They remain in memory, so export before closing. Turning “something felt strange a moment ago” into a record I can revisit is already a worthwhile reason to make the tool.']},
 {title:'A new signal in the collection',paragraphs:['The website exhibition borrows the app’s exact palette, fonts and metal frames. Hardware panels become a little floating instrument assembly. CPU, GPU and RAM controls change the colour and feature perspective; below, you can browse actual Simple, PRO and Mini screenshots. The animated waveforms are artwork, and the exhibition never reads a visitor’s hardware.','CHROMA 0.1.0 is on GitHub with Windows installer and portable builds. The interface, documentation and this journal are available in Chinese and English. Setup explains the optional enhanced CPU sensor service. The current binaries are unsigned, and the repository records what has and has not been verified.','From a basketball game to everyday utilities, the things I like are finding places of their own. This time, it is the computer itself. Its signals deserve a little of my favourite colour, too.'],image:'./assets/projects/chroma/cover.svg',imageAlt:'Original CHROMA Y2K poster with blue, yellow, red, a chrome monitor and burst stars',caption:'SIGNAL REMIX / 006. Original artwork using CHROMA’s colour, typography and instrument-panel character.'}],
 cta:'Enter the CHROMA observatory',afterword:'Written as CHROMA 0.1.0 joins the collection. Stay curious. Give every signal some colour.'
 }
